 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Galbi-jjim is even better the next day.</li><li>Cool it and refrigerate it in the pan or a container.</li><li>The next day, lift off the hardened fat and gently reheat it covered with a splash of water before reducing it to a glaze.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Make it a day ahead"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Asian pear (nashi) has an enzyme that softens the meat and makes the sauce fruity.</li><li>A crisp apple is the easiest substitute; kiwi works too but only use ¼ of one, because too much makes the meat mushy.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Pear is the tenderiser"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>If your lid lets out a lot of steam, put a sheet of baking paper under it and check the liquid every 15 minutes.</li><li>If you only have a loose lid, the slow part can be done in the oven at <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"temp\" data-u=\"C\" data-a=\"160\" data-s=\"0\">160 °C</span> in an oven-safe pan.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Lid fit matters"
  }
 ],
